DIAGNOSTIC D-BEN: PROGRAM-LEVEL DECOMPOSITION OF THE TWO-L / F3 BENEFIT WOBBLE.
Read-only forensic. No welfare, no decomposition, no V_i, no estimation, no
EUROMOD SYSTEM edits, no fix applied, no promotion, no commit. ONE report + one
diagnostic parquet. Purpose: attribute the F3 joint-batch ils_ben divergence
(observed EUR 127-310 on means-tested benefits) to SPECIFIC ils_benmt programs
and classify each moving program as INCOME-DRIVEN (real response to the draw's
earnings) vs DEMOGRAPHIC (should not vary with decider hours -> batch artifact).

CONTEXT (verify, do not assume)
The F3 joint-batch parity gate found: ils_origy / ils_tax / ils_sicdy reproduce
to 0.00 across batch composition; ils_ben diverged EUR 127-310; divergence was
confined to benefit-receiving counterfactual nodes. This diagnostic opens the
ils_ben aggregate to the program level. ils_benmt is a SUM of ~15 programs
(bchyc_s PAJE, bsuwd_s AV, bunmt_s ASS, bchlg_s CF, bched_s ARS, bchba_s PN,
bsaoa_s ASPA, bdi_s AAH, bchlp_s API, bsa00_s RSA, bhotn_s AL-housing, tinrf_s
PPE, bchot_s, bsaot, bhoot, bed, ...). ils_bennt and ils_pen are separate sums.
The decider choice vars that change per draw are lhw, yivwg, and the rebuilt
yem00/yemxp/yem; everything else (incl. all household demographics: children,
ages, disability flags) is replicated UNCHANGED across the 101 draws.

TASK 0 — REPRODUCE THE FAILING CONTRAST EXACTLY.
Re-run the SAME comparison F3 ran that produced the EUR 127-310: the production-
geometry batch vs the alternative-composition batch (state precisely which two
batch constructions are being compared, citing the F3 runner / config). Confirm
you reproduce the ils_ben divergence magnitude on the SAME households/draws F3
flagged. If you cannot reproduce it, STOP and report why (the diagnostic is
meaningless on a contrast that doesn't reproduce). Pull the per-program EUROMOD
outputs (every ils_benmt component listed above, plus ils_bennt and ils_pen
totals) for BOTH batch constructions, same households, same draws.

TASK 1 — PER-PROGRAM ATTRIBUTION OF THE DIVERGENCE.
For each ils_benmt program column present in the EUROMOD output, compute across
the flagged nodes: max abs diff, median abs diff, n_nodes_above_tol (tol 1e-6),
and the program's share of the total ils_ben divergence. Produce a ranked table:
which programs carry the EUR 127-310, in order. Confirm the per-program diffs
sum to the ils_ben aggregate diff (closure check). Also confirm ils_bennt and
ils_pen do NOT move (expected; report if they do).

TASK 2 — CLASSIFY EACH MOVING PROGRAM (the branch-point).
For every program with nonzero divergence, classify it as:
  INCOME-DRIVEN (real): eligibility/amount depends on the household's earnings,
    so it legitimately responds to the draw's lhw/yivwg/yem. Expected members:
    bsa00_s (RSA), bhotn_s/bhoot (housing), bunmt_s (ASS), tinrf_s (PPE), and
    any income-tested family/disability line (bdi_s AAH, bched_s ARS if income-
    tested). State the basis for each call (does its amount track earnings in the
    EUROMOD FR rules / does it move WITH the draw's earnings across draws within
    a single faithful run?).
  DEMOGRAPHIC (artifact-suspect): amount is set by household composition
    (children count/age, disability), NOT decider hours. Expected: bch00-type
    family lines, bchyc_s PAJE, bchlg_s CF, bchba_s PN. For these, the per-draw
    value SHOULD be identical across the 101 draws (demographics fixed); if such
    a program is MOVING across batch composition, that is a BATCH ARTIFACT, not
    economic response.
  Decision evidence to compute per program: (a) within a SINGLE faithful run,
    does the program vary across the 101 draws? (varies => income-linked; constant
    => demographic). (b) across the TWO batch constructions at FIXED draw, does it
    move? (moves => batch-sensitive). The cross of these two is the classifier:
       varies-across-draws + batch-sensitive  -> INCOME-DRIVEN (real; do NOT freeze)
       constant-across-draws + batch-sensitive -> DEMOGRAPHIC ARTIFACT (freeze candidate)
       constant + not-batch-sensitive          -> inert (no action)

TASK 3 — WITHIN- vs CROSS-HOUSEHOLD DEPENDENCE (precision the fix depends on).
State explicitly, per moving program, whether its batch sensitivity is:
  - WITHIN-household legitimate (depends on this household's own members — e.g. a
    child triggering a family benefit): REAL, must be preserved by any fix; or
  - CROSS-household leakage (this household's program shifts because OTHER
    households' composition in the batch changed): the Two-L pathology, the only
    thing a fix should target.
Do not conflate them. A fix that touches within-household eligibility would break
the model; a fix must target only cross-household batch normalisation.

TASK 4 — FIX-OPTION READOUT (analysis only; implement nothing).
Given Tasks 1-3, state which of the operator's three options each finding supports:
  A (faithful full pre-filtration population run): supported if divergence is
    income-driven and a single faithful population reproduces production ils_ben.
  B (per-household isolated full run): the validated gold standard; note cost.
  C (freeze specific programs to data-level in a new EUROMOD system): legal ONLY
    for programs classified DEMOGRAPHIC ARTIFACT + CROSS-household; ILLEGAL for any
    INCOME-DRIVEN program (freezing those deletes the means-tested response to the
    counterfactual wage — the consumption-floor mechanism). List, by name, which
    programs are freeze-legal and which are freeze-illegal.
Also report, for magnitude context (NOT a welfare computation): the EUR 127-310
divergence as a fraction of the affected nodes' ils_dispy, so the operator can
see whether the wobble is even large relative to disposable income.

